    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "On the libertarian side, Robert Nozick's \"Entitlement Theory\" (1974) provides a rigorously philosophical argument against high taxation as a moral violation. Nozick's key insight is that individuals have \"entitlements\" to their property, which are prior to any external conquest or imposition. He argues that taxation infringes upon these entitlements by taking property without consent, thereby violating the principle of justificability. \n\nThis argument is rooted in a moral theory known as \" Locke-Social Contractism\", but also draws heavily from libertarian moral philosophers like John Romand and Isiah Shirley. This approach asserts that government actions can be morally justified only if they are deemed analogous to, or consistent with, the tacit consent of the individual citizens from whom they are taking property.\n\nMoreover, Nozick puts forth the idea that just taxation should be closely related to reparations, where individuals receive compensation in the form of reduced economic burdens when the government advances property rights and the common good. This prevents higher-level utilitarian calculations are made, and applies the Views of Enttiny Principle, where an individual's rescuing own property will usually be better off (assuming error tolerance when factasse determinant producing attribute by biased vibes hegegrimp-pr",
